<!--
  Loop indoor scenario: 50m x 50m floor plan, 3m walls (open top).

  Coordinate system: the building centre is at (0, 0, 0); the floor spans
  x/y = -25 to +25, matching large_indoor so mission figures compare directly.

      +25 +-------------------------------------------+
          | NW-corner |      N-outer       | NE-corner |
          |-----D-----D--------------------D-----D-----|
          |     |  +=========================+  |      |
          |     |  |  NW-core  |  NE-core    |  |      |
          |  W  |  |     +-----D-----+       |  |   E  |   ring corridor
          | out D  D-----+  cross    +-------D  D  out  |   (3.2 m, closed)
          |  er |  |     +-----D-----+       |  |   er  |
          |     |  |  SW-core  |  SE-core    |  |      |
          |     |  +=========================+  |      |
          |-----D-----D--------------------D-----D-----|
          | SW-corner |      S-outer       | SE-corner |
      -25 +-------------------------------------------+

  WHY THIS SCENE EXISTS — LOOP CONNECTIVITY.

  large_indoor is a corridor *cross*: one central junction, four quadrants.
  Measured drone paths there show long-gap revisits (a drone leaves a region
  and comes back 600-1000 ticks later), about half of them inside the corridor
  itself. With a single chokepoint that may be forced by the floor plan rather
  than chosen by the frontier strategy: a drone that explores the north-east
  and then the south-west *has* to cross the junction twice.

  This scene is the control that separates the two explanations. Every region
  has at least two genuinely distinct routes to every other, and no single cell
  disconnects the map:

    * a closed ring corridor runs right around the building, so any two points
      on it have a clockwise and an anticlockwise route;
    * two cross-corridors chord the ring through the centre, adding a third
      and fourth circulation route;
    * every room has TWO doorways. The four outer side rooms open twice onto
      the ring; the four corner rooms open onto the two side rooms flanking
      them (closing an outer loop); the four core rooms open once onto the ring
      and once onto a cross-corridor.

  If long-gap revisits collapse here, the corridor cross was the bottleneck.
  If they persist, the frontier strategy is choosing targets badly. A layout
  that merely rearranged large_indoor's rooms would answer nothing.

  DIMENSIONS ARE NOT ARBITRARY, and follow large_indoor exactly. Feature 4c
  measured that a gap must clear `2r + 1` cells PLUS one cell of discretization
  slop: a wall face landing on a cell boundary has its ray hit point attributed
  to the far cell, so the mapped gap is one cell narrower than the real one. At
  resolution 0.2 with clearance_radius 0.20 (r = 1) that floor is 4 cells /
  0.8 m. Doorways here are 6 cells / 1.2 m and corridors 16 cells / 3.2 m, so
  the scene survives clearance_radius rising to r = 2.

  EVERY WALL FACE LIES ON A 0.2 m LATTICE POINT, which is also a 0.1 m lattice
  point. A face at a half-cell offset is exactly what turned a 3-cell gap into
  2 free cells in the 4c measurement. Geometry was generated from exact
  rational arithmetic (fractions.Fraction) rather than typed, and
  tests/unit/test_simulation/test_loop_indoor.py re-asserts the lattice
  invariant against the parsed model.

  Key lines, all on the lattice:
    perimeter wall      centre +-25.0   (inner face +-24.8)
    outer ring wall     centre +-16.2   (faces 16.0 / 16.4)
    inner ring wall     centre +-12.6   (faces 12.4 / 12.8)
    cross-corridor wall centre +- 1.8   (faces  1.6 /  2.0)
  giving a ring corridor 16.0 - 12.8 = 3.2 m wide and cross-corridors 3.2 m.

  OBSTACLE HEIGHTS VARY DELIBERATELY. The drone flies at 0.3 m and the sensor
  has an upward elevation fan, so height is information the 2.5D map records.
  large_indoor's crates are all 0.8-1.2 m tall, which barely exercises it.
  Tops here are 0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 1.6, 2.2 and 2.4 m — one below the flight
  altitude (invisible to an upward fan, and a free negative control), the rest
  spread up to nearly wall height.

  Box geom sizes are half-extents. Walls are 0.4 m thick (half-extent 0.2).

  Drones are injected programmatically by SimulationEngine —
  do not define drone bodies here.

  Rendering follows large_indoor.xml: open top (no ceiling geom) so --view
  looks down into the plan, opaque walls, two directional lights. The
  rangefinder never casts above the wall tops at this altitude, so omitting a
  ceiling leaves the produced map identical.
-->
<mujoco model="loop_indoor">
  <option timestep="0.01" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <!-- Default free-camera framing, as in large_indoor.xml: the passive viewer
       otherwise opens at floor level. -->
  <statistic center="0 0 1" extent="45"/>
  <visual>
    <headlight ambient="0.5 0.5 0.5" diffuse="0.45 0.45 0.45" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <global azimuth="130" elevation="-55"/>
  </visual>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Directional lights: no positional falloff, so a 50 m floor has no
         hotspot. Lights never collide — no effect on ray-casts or the map. -->
    <light name="key" directional="true" pos="0 0 20" dir="0.2 0.3 -1"
           diffuse="0.55 0.55 0.55" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <light name="fill" directional="true" pos="15 15 20" dir="-0.5 -0.5 -1"
           diffuse="0.35 0.35 0.35" specular="0 0 0"/>

    <!-- Floor -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="25 25 0.05"
          rgba="0.75 0.75 0.78 1.0"/>

    <!-- Perimeter (generated; see the header) -->
    <geom name="wall_north" type="box" pos="0 25 1.5"
          size="25 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_south" type="box" pos="0 -25 1.5"
          size="25 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_east" type="box" pos="25 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 25 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_west" type="box" pos="-25 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 25 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- Outer ring wall: side rooms outside it, racetrack corridor inside.
         Doors at x/y = +-8 join each side room to the ring (two each);
         doors at +-20.6 join each corner room to its two neighbours,
         which is what closes the outer loop. -->
    <geom name="ring_n_1" type="box" pos="-23.1 16.2 1.5"
          size="1.9 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_n_2" type="box" pos="-14.3 16.2 1.5"
          size="5.7 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_n_3" type="box" pos="0 16.2 1.5"
          size="7.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_n_4" type="box" pos="14.3 16.2 1.5"
          size="5.7 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_n_5" type="box" pos="23.1 16.2 1.5"
          size="1.9 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_s_1" type="box" pos="-23.1 -16.2 1.5"
          size="1.9 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_s_2" type="box" pos="-14.3 -16.2 1.5"
          size="5.7 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_s_3" type="box" pos="0 -16.2 1.5"
          size="7.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_s_4" type="box" pos="14.3 -16.2 1.5"
          size="5.7 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_s_5" type="box" pos="23.1 -16.2 1.5"
          size="1.9 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_e_1" type="box" pos="16.2 -23.1 1.5"
          size="0.2 1.9 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_e_2" type="box" pos="16.2 -14.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.7 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_e_3" type="box" pos="16.2 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 7.4 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_e_4" type="box" pos="16.2 14.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.7 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_e_5" type="box" pos="16.2 23.1 1.5"
          size="0.2 1.9 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_w_1" type="box" pos="-16.2 -23.1 1.5"
          size="0.2 1.9 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_w_2" type="box" pos="-16.2 -14.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.7 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_w_3" type="box" pos="-16.2 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 7.4 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_w_4" type="box" pos="-16.2 14.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.7 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="ring_w_5" type="box" pos="-16.2 23.1 1.5"
          size="0.2 1.9 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- Inner ring wall: corridor outside it, core rooms inside. The
         north/south runs carry the cross-corridor mouth plus one door
         per core room; east/west carry the mouth only. -->
    <geom name="core_n_1" type="box" pos="-10.3 12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_n_2" type="box" pos="-4.1 12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_n_3" type="box" pos="4.1 12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_n_4" type="box" pos="10.3 12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_s_1" type="box" pos="-10.3 -12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_s_2" type="box" pos="-4.1 -12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_s_3" type="box" pos="4.1 -12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_s_4" type="box" pos="10.3 -12.6 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_e_1" type="box" pos="12.6 -7.2 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.6 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_e_2" type="box" pos="12.6 7.2 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.6 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_w_1" type="box" pos="-12.6 -7.2 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.6 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="core_w_2" type="box" pos="-12.6 7.2 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.6 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- Cross-corridor walls: the two chords through the centre. -->
    <geom name="cross_e_1" type="box" pos="1.8 7.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_e_2" type="box" pos="1.8 -7.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_w_1" type="box" pos="-1.8 7.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_w_2" type="box" pos="-1.8 -7.3 1.5"
          size="0.2 5.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_n_1" type="box" pos="4.2 1.8 1.5"
          size="2.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_n_2" type="box" pos="10.3 1.8 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_n_3" type="box" pos="-10.3 1.8 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_n_4" type="box" pos="-4.2 1.8 1.5"
          size="2.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_s_1" type="box" pos="4.2 -1.8 1.5"
          size="2.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_s_2" type="box" pos="10.3 -1.8 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_s_3" type="box" pos="-10.3 -1.8 1.5"
          size="2.5 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cross_s_4" type="box" pos="-4.2 -1.8 1.5"
          size="2.4 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- Obstacles. Tops at 0.2 / 0.5 / 0.9 / 1.6 / 2.2 / 2.4 m; see the
         header on why the spread matters at a 0.3 m flight altitude. -->
    <geom name="pallet_n" type="box" pos="-8 20.4 0.1"
          size="1.2 1 0.1" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
    <geom name="crate_e" type="box" pos="20.4 8 0.25"
          size="1 1 0.25" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
    <geom name="crate_s" type="box" pos="-6 -20.4 0.45"
          size="1.2 0.8 0.45" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
    <geom name="shelf_w" type="box" pos="-20.4 -8 0.8"
          size="0.8 1.6 0.8" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
    <geom name="pillar_ne" type="box" pos="10 4 1.1"
          size="0.8 0.8 1.1" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
    <geom name="pillar_sw" type="box" pos="-10 -4 1.2"
          size="0.8 0.8 1.2" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1.0"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
